 "intro_html": "Tokyo's onigiri specialists sell dozens of fillings. <b>Onigiri Bongo</b> in Kita-Ōtsuka, open since 1960, has about 55, and people queue for hours. <b>Asakusa Yadoroku</b>, the first onigiri shop with a Michelin Bib Gourmand, keeps fewer than 20 traditional ones. These are home versions of the ones you can cook, plus a list of the ones you just buy. The shops don't publish recipes, so the amounts are this site's own. Each filling makes 4–6 onigiri: pick two or three. Rice, salt and shaping are on the <a href=\"onigiri.html\">onigiri page</a>; the last step shows how the shops fill them.",
