 "notes": [
  {
   "html": "<ul class=\"do\"><li>Bongo's signature is <b>sujiko</b>, salt-cured salmon roe still in its membrane.</li><li>Other fillings you just buy: <b>negitoro</b> (chopped fatty tuna with scallion, eat within the hour), <b>salmon with mentaiko</b> on top, <b>grilled tarako with mentai mayo</b> (Togoshi-ya), and Yadoroku's <b>kazunoko in sake lees</b> (herring roe) and <b>ami tsukudani</b> (tiny shrimp simmered in sweet soy).</li></ul>",
   "title_html": "Buy and fill"
  },
  {
   "html": "<ul class=\"do\"><li>In spring Bongo also has fuki miso, butterbur stems chopped and fried with miso and sugar.</li><li>The peanut miso in step 6 is the year-round cousin.</li></ul>",
   "title_html": "Fuki miso"
  },
  {
   "html": "<ul class=\"do\"><li>Nigihaya in Kyoto uses its family's Marizuke, eggplant salted for two years and then simmered.</li><li>Step 7 is a quick version of the idea, not their recipe.</li></ul>",
   "title_html": "Nigihaya's eggplant"
  },
  {
   "html": "<ul class=\"do\"><li>Soboro, shiso kombu, takana and peanut miso keep 4–5 days in the fridge.</li><li>Pork kimchi keeps two days.</li></ul>",
   "title_html": "Keeping"
  }
 ],
